<p>Google just moved an approval gate from a person to a paragraph of rules. I haven&rsquo;t used the product; this is a reading of <a href="https://developers.googleblog.com/en/check-what-your-ai-agents-do-before-they-do-it-with-semantic-governance-policies/">Google&rsquo;s launch post</a> and its docs, set against the maturity model on this site. The feature is in Preview on Google&rsquo;s Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, and it&rsquo;s enforced at a component called Agent Gateway. My read is that it&rsquo;s a real step, with one half of the job missing.</p>

<h2>What does it check?</h2>

<p>Every tool call the model proposes gets checked before it runs. The check has two parts: plain-language rules you write per agent (for all tools or for one), and what the user actually asked for. That second part, the intent check, is on by default. In Google&rsquo;s words, &ldquo;A call has to pass both checks to run.&rdquo;</p>

<p>The user&rsquo;s request and the system instructions are trusted. Tool results, files, and messages are untrusted data. That split targets prompt injection, where text hidden in a web page or email tells the agent to do something its user never asked for. Google&rsquo;s <a href="https://developers.googleblog.com/did-the-user-ask-for-this-securing-ai-agents-against-intent-hijacking-with-semantic-governance-policies/">second post</a> calls the result intent hijacking: the agent&rsquo;s goal gets swapped mid-task.</p>

<p>The numbers there are strong. AgentDojo is a public benchmark that plants malicious instructions in an agent&rsquo;s tool results to see whether the agent follows them. With Google&rsquo;s Gemini 2.5 Flash model as the agent, 97 user tasks, and 949 attacked runs (six full runs, averaged), attack success fell from 48.7% to 0.9%. Run the arithmetic: 48.7% of 949 is about 462 successful hijacks per run; 0.9% is 8 or 9. Google says none of the 929 attacks that needed a tool call got through in any run.</p>

<h2>Why does it matter?</h2>

<p>It moves the gate. Instead of a confirmation prompt on every call, you answer the questions once, in writing, and the policy answers them again every time. That&rsquo;s the shape a Level 5 system needs: bounded, without stopping to ask. I&rsquo;ve argued before that <a href="/blog/question-at-the-start">a question at the start isn&rsquo;t a handoff</a>, and with <a href="/blog/dots-and-the-approval-dial">OpenAI&rsquo;s dots and the approval dial</a> I looked at where the human sits by default. A standing policy takes the human out of the per-call loop; that&rsquo;s the point of <a href="/blog/human-handoff-problem">The Human Handoff Problem</a>.</p>

<h2>Who judges?</h2>

<p>A large language model (LLM) judges the call the agent&rsquo;s own model proposed. The judge sees the conversation so far, the agent&rsquo;s system instructions and tool descriptions, and your policies, then returns allow or deny. Google doesn&rsquo;t name the judge model. So I can&rsquo;t say whether it shares blind spots with the agent it&rsquo;s checking. I wrote about that problem in <a href="/blog/when-one-model-checks-another">When one model checks another</a>.</p>

<p>The trust line deserves a flag too. Google says it&rsquo;s &ldquo;enforced by how the judge is instructed, not by a hard technical boundary.&rdquo; I think that&rsquo;s an honest sentence, and it means the wall is an instruction.</p>

<h2>Then what?</h2>

<p>Here&rsquo;s where the description stops. A denied call is replaced, and Google says &ldquo;In each denied case, the agent gets a denial message instead of the call.&rdquo; Cloud Logging, Google&rsquo;s logging service, records each verdict, the judge&rsquo;s rationale, and tokens used. Nothing I read says what the agent does next. No retry, replan, or escalation to a human is described.</p>

<p>Does it try a narrower call? Report back? Quit? Take the numbers. In the benchmark, about 1 of 97 harmless tasks was over-blocked, and benign-task success fell by roughly 2.6 points. This site runs 104 publish cycles a year (2 per week times 52); at about 1%, that&rsquo;s one wrongly blocked publish a year. On that one day, a deny with no next move turns a safety check into a silent stall. That&rsquo;s the recovery half, and <a href="/blog/recovery-is-more-than-a-retry">recovery is more than a retry</a>.</p>

<h2>Dry run?</h2>

<p>Credit where it&rsquo;s due: dry run mode logs what the engine would deny without blocking anything, and Google recommends reviewing it before enforcing. That&rsquo;s the right order, observation before enforcement. You get to look before the gate acts.</p>

<h2>Side door?</h2>

<p>There&rsquo;s a gap, though. Google states plainly that &ldquo;Streamed model responses aren&rsquo;t evaluated.&rdquo; Text-only responses aren&rsquo;t evaluated either, since they take no action. A gate with a side door is only as good as your knowledge of the door. My read is that any agent path that streams is where I&rsquo;d look first in a deployment.</p>

<h2>Where does it sit?</h2>

<p>It&rsquo;s a component, not a system, so I wouldn&rsquo;t place it on the <a href="/maturity">maturity page</a> at all. It does support bounded autonomy without handoffs, which the <a href="/evaluation">evaluation framework</a> requires. But it contributes nothing to completion determination, and nothing to recovery.</p>

<h2>Fair?</h2>

<p>Am I grading a launch post like a spec? Google publishes its own caveats: AgentDojo&rsquo;s attacks are fixed and weren&rsquo;t adapted to the policies, so the results don&rsquo;t measure adaptive attackers. And &ldquo;Because verdicts come from an LLM, which is probabilistic, they may vary.&rdquo; Turning intent matching off also reduces protection against indirect prompt injection. No price is published. A launch post isn&rsquo;t a spec; but the gaps I&rsquo;m pointing at are ones the post leaves open, not ones it hides.</p>

<h2>What can you test?</h2>

<p>Run it in dry run against your own agent&rsquo;s last week of traffic. Count the denies, then read the rationales; that tells you whether the judge understands your tools. Then deliberately trigger a deny and watch what the agent does next. If it stalls, you&rsquo;ve learned something no benchmark will tell you.</p>

<p>A gate that says no is half a design; the other half is what the agent does after it hears it.</p>

